{"format":1,"seq":497,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to switch the parity check to use the durable extension's hook task, then start writing tests—updating the context-planning hook tests with collapse planning coverage, plus context.test.ts integration tests and harness support for failed runs. I'm setting up the imports for the apply-edits hook and collapse planner in the context-planning test file.\n\nNow I'm constructing a test for the apply-edits hook that verifies it requests a task even when nothing is staged, as long as collapse settings are present. I'm also building a helper to simulate a finished run with assistant blocks and tool results, then exercising planCollapse against it to confirm the current run's entries get skipped when they're at or past the \"first\" cursor—checking that a collapsed typescript call produces an exact expected summary line with the right label, workspace methods used, status, token count, and original entry references.\n\nI'm checking variations: unlabelled calls default to using the first line of code as a title, failed calls should surface the error message in the outcome, non-typescript tool calls produce a simpler collapsed line, and multiple results in one assistant turn should combine into a \"collapsed N tool calls\" summary line.\n\nI also need a test enforcing the byte-length bound — ensuring the line stays within 512 bytes even with huge labels or many errors, that it still preserves the IDs and drops function details, and that multibyte characters like emoji get truncated safely on code point boundaries without leaving stray replacement characters. Then there's a planning test to verify that answers, user messages, and kept runs are protected from collapsing.\n\nI should add a planning test confirming only finished runs past the compaction head get collapsed, respecting the keepRuns boundary, plus a case where a tiny call under the token threshold stays uncollapsed. For the integration suite, I need to extend the host() function to accept extension options, and add test programs with labeled bulky output.\n\nI also want to simulate a failed run in the harness — adding a fourth parameter to run() for options like `{ answer: \"error\" }`, implemented by returning a faux assistant message with an error stop reason, and disabling retry in settings so the failure surfaces directly. I need to verify fauxAssistantMessage actually supports an errorMessage option before relying on it.\n\nWriting integration test code...\n\nI'm now setting up a test case where the durable extension collapses finished runs' tool calls down to their labels, while preserving the newest run plus the answers and user messages—running two sequential conversation turns to verify this behavior.\n\nThen I'm checking a forked conversation after a third run: confirming the collapsed labels only include the first two runs, the active tool call label is the third run's, the text content reflects the correct labels without duplication, user messages accumulate across turns, and the answers/tool call pairings remain intact after forking.\n\nStill writing inspect program, filtering collapsed entries and extracting original IDs via regex, mindful of escaping backslashes correctly inside the TS template literal.\n\nI'm checking that the states come out elided for both entries and that the originals contain the expected call labels and repeated text. Then I move to verifying idempotence after the inspect run by checking the context-edit entries.\n\nI'm asserting that every covered id appears in exactly one collapse operation, and that there are exactly three context-edit entries after runs 2 through 4. Then I set up a parametrized test covering cases where nothing should collapse, either because minTokens is set impossibly high or collapse is disabled entirely, running two iterations and checking messages stay intact.\n\nFor the failure scenario, I want a harness where a program stages elision but the final model response errors out, verifying the run fails cleanly with no collapse, no elide stub, and no context-edit entries recorded. Then I move to testing model-triggered elision within the same run, setting up multiple labeled runs where a program elides an earlier result mid-run.\n\nAfter that run, I'd expect exactly one context-edit entry containing both elide and collapse operations, with the collapsed range excluding the elided call's ids—messages should show the elided call verbatim but its result replaced by a stub, while later runs stay intact per the keepRuns setting. I also want to check the keepRuns boundary carefully, confirming a kept run stays whole and only one edit entry total gets created, and think about mutation targets for the probe—adjusting the keepRuns math and verifying that answers without tool calls are never candidates for collapsing.\n\nTracing through protection logic, user messages aren't assistant role so they're excluded structurally before the toolCallIds check even matters; if I mutate the role check away, user and answer items with empty toolCallIds would need the length check to still catch them, and answers would end up marked as untouched with a \"collapsed 0 tool calls\" note.\n\nThe real gap is that the test's faux answer text (\"done\") is too small to matter for the token-size collapse comparison, so the mutation might survive undetected. I should add a large answer text to the planning test history so that removing the toolCallIds check would actually cause a visible collapse, killing the mutant — now I need to check the fauxAssistantMessage options to implement this.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01JsitjEAat3NJWfwH1Q7aoU","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Check fauxAssistantMessage options signature\"}\nasync ({ shell: { exec } }) => (await exec(\"grep -n 'export declare function fauxAssistantM